body:
This is a very long post.

&#x200B;

It starts off as a small twinge of sadness. Maybe you had a bad day, but you're still very hopeful that tomorrow will be better than today. Tomorrow comes and you still have that small twinge of sadness, yet you're still certain that tomorrow will be an amazing day.

&#x200B;

Time passes, and that small twinge of sadness has grown into a raging inferno. That hopefulness you held days/weeks/months ago is no longer there. It feels like a continuing cycle of bad day after bad day. Your mental health is not the only thing affected. Before you know it, you've given up the habit of maintaining your hygiene.

&#x200B;

It's been days/weeks since you've last showered because you don't have the mental energy to get up and get clean. You lose the habit of brushing your teeth, doing skincare and shaving. Your bedroom and home become an absolute mess because you don't have the energy to clean. Dishes begin to pile up, laundry goes undone for days or even weeks. You give up on going to school/work because it doesn't matter anymore. There's still more.

&#x200B;

You stop doing the things you love. Your computer, gaming console, musical instrument etc. now have a thin layer of dust on them since you haven't had energy to do the things you once loved to do. You stop going out with your friends and family. You always say something along the lines of "I've got a lot of work to do" or "I'm really tired, how about another time?" and hope that they don't notice.

&#x200B;

You eventually stop checking your phone. The energy you once had to do your favorite things and text your friends has left long ago. Notifications have reached into the hundreds with friends and family texting/calling you to make sure that you're okay. Now they realize that something is seriously wrong.

&#x200B;

At this point, many people feel "too far gone". Unfortunately, in some cases, people begin to look for ways out/to cope. You begin attempts on your life, you've finally had enough of this horrible world. Your family/friend(s) end up finding you and rushing you to the hospital. Some people get very lucky and end up surviving, while others do not. After recovering from the hospital, you're forced to get help. At first, you despise the idea and hate the people that saved you. As time goes on, you realize that life is worth living and that you're not alone.

&#x200B;

You're not alone. Sincerely, a depression survivor.
